<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.00%" pitch="-2.60%">
            In those days a decree goes out from Caesar Augustus that all the world should be registered, and Joseph goes up from Galilee to Judea, to the city of David called Bethlehem.<break time="500ms"/>
      Mary goes with him, because she is betrothed to Joseph and is with child, and the imperial decree becomes the road by which Scripture's place is quietly met.<break time="500ms"/>
      Luke does not dramatize the journey with extra detail; he simply shows the obedience of ordinary travel under the providence of God.<break time="1000ms"/>
      They arrive in Bethlehem, and while they are there, the time comes for her to give birth.<break time="500ms"/>
      The story moves with surprising stillness: a long promise, a government decree, a journey, and then the hour appointed by God.<break time="500ms"/>
      Even before the child is laid in a manger, the nativity shows that God works through roads, timings, and places that human beings do not fully arrange.<break time="1800ms"/>
      Nothing in this scene is outside God's rule.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Bethlehem is reached in the ordinary movement of travel and time.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The Lord guides history toward His promise without strain.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You do not need to manage every road for God to be faithful on it.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>